  <section id="short-answer">
    <h2>The short answer</h2>
    <p>
      It depends on which river you are on and which country you are in. There is no single "paddleboard licence" that applies everywhere. What exists is a patchwork: some navigations in England and Wales require a licence or registration for any boat, including a stand up paddleboard (SUP) or kayak; Scotland has no such system; and tidal and coastal water works differently again.
    </p>
    <p>
      This guide explains the patchwork in plain terms, so you can work out what applies to the stretch you actually plan to paddle. It is general information, not legal advice, and rules change, so always confirm with the body that manages your river before you go.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="england-wales">
    <h2>England and Wales: navigation authorities and Paddle UK</h2>
    <p>
      Many rivers in England and Wales are managed by a navigation authority, usually the <a href="https://canalrivertrust.org.uk/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Canal &amp; River Trust</a>, the <a href="https://www.gov.uk/guidance/boating-licences-and-registration" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Environment Agency</a> or the Broads Authority. On waters they manage, boats generally need to be licensed or registered. That includes human-powered craft such as paddleboards, canoes and kayaks, although the cost for these small craft is much lower than for motor boats.
    </p>
    <p>
      The practical shortcut is membership of Paddle UK (the body formerly known as British Canoeing). Its "On the Water" membership includes a waterways licence for paddlers, which Paddle UK describes as covering around 4,500 km of canals and rivers managed by the Canal &amp; River Trust, the Environment Agency, the Broads Authority and a number of other navigation authorities. If you paddle several rivers, membership is usually simpler and better value than buying individual licences.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two things to keep in mind. First, the licence is for the water, not for the sport: membership does not give you the right to paddle across private land to reach a river, or on stretches that are not covered by it. Second, there is also a separate question of public rights of navigation. Some rivers have a clear legal right for the public to navigate, others do not, and many stretches fall into a grey area where the landowner's permission matters. Paddle UK publishes access information that is worth reading for any river you do not know.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="scotland">
    <h2>Scotland: no licence, but responsibilities</h2>
    <p>
      In Scotland there is no equivalent licence for paddling on rivers and lochs. The Land Reform (Scotland) Act 2003 gives people a statutory right of responsible access to most land and inland water for recreation, including paddling. The <a href="https://www.outdooraccess-scotland.scot/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Scottish Outdoor Access Code</a> sets out what responsible means in practice: respect people's privacy and peace, care for the environment, and take responsibility for your own safety.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Caledonian Canal and some other managed waterways have their own rules and fees, so check the managing body if you plan to paddle one of them. Salmon rivers also have fishing seasons and anglers to share the water with, which is a matter of courtesy as much as rules.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="how-to-check">
    <h2>How to check the rules for a specific river</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Find out who manages the stretch. The river page for most rivers on this site names the main regulator, and the Canal &amp; River Trust and Environment Agency both publish navigation maps.</li>
      <li>Check whether it is a navigation. If it is, ask whether Paddle UK membership or a day licence covers it.</li>
      <li>Check the access points. Even where you may paddle, you still need a legal way to reach the water: a public launch site, a public right of way or the landowner's permission.</li>
      <li>Look for local restrictions. Weirs, locks, bird-nesting areas, fishing beats and water company intakes can all change where and when you can go.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

