  <section id="access">
    <h2>Access, landowners and etiquette</h2>
    <p>
      Licences answer "may I paddle here?" Access answers "may I get to the river and launch?" In England and Wales you generally cannot rely on a right to launch from any bank, because the land alongside the water is often private. Use official launch points, canoe clubs that grant access to members and guests, and public car parks and footpaths that reach the water.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Give way to anglers and pass them wide and slowly. Do not paddle over lines.</li>
      <li>Keep your distance from nesting birds, especially in spring. Swans, herons and kingfishers all use river banks.</li>
      <li>Clean, drain and dry your board and kit between rivers to avoid spreading invasive species. The Non-native Species Secretariat's Check Clean Dry campaign explains how.</li>
      <li>Take litter home, including any you find.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="before-you-go">
    <h2>Before you launch</h2>
    <p>
      Rules are only part of preparing for a river. Currents, weirs, strainers (fallen trees) and cold water are the things that injure people. Check the level and forecast, wear a buoyancy aid and a leash that suits flowing water, tell someone your plan, and read our guide to <a href="/blog/river-kayaking-safety-for-beginners/">river safety for beginners</a>. If you are new, a session with a qualified coach on a calm stretch is the best money you can spend.
    </p>
    <p>
      Browse <a href="/rivers/">our river guides</a> to see which regulator looks after each river and what the main hazards are.
    </p>
  </section>

